@end defun

@defun descendentSequence (Sequence*, bool close) @arrow{} Sequence*
Creates a descendant Sequence of a Sequence. Those in the input Sequence
are not in the descendents unless they are a descendent of someone in the input Sequence.

TODO: Consider adding a "close" flag to the interface.
@end defun

@defun siblingSequence (Sequence*, bool close) @arrow{} Sequence*
Creates the Sequence of the siblings of the persons in the input
Sequence.  If @code{close} is @code{true}, persons in the input
Sequence are added to the sibling Sequence.
@end defun

@defun childSequence (Sequence*) @arrow{} Sequence*
Creates a Sequence of the children of the persons in another Sequence.
@end defun

@defun parentSequence (Sequence*) @arrow{} Sequence*
Creates a Sequence with the parents of the persons in given Sequence.
@end defun

@defun spouseSequence (Sequence*) @arrow{} Sequence*
Creates spouses Sequence of a Sequence.
@end defun

@defun sequenceToGedcom (Sequence*, FILE*) @arrow{} void
Generate a Gedcom file from a sequence of persons. Only persons in the
the sequence are written. Families with links to at least two persons
in the sequence are also written to the file. Other persons referred
to in the families are not included, and the links to the other
persons are removed.
@end defun

@defun limitPersonNode (GNode* node, int level) @arrow{} bool
@end defun

@defun familyToSpouses (GNode* fam, Database* database) @arrow{} Sequence*
Create sequence of spouses of family.
@end defun

@defun nameToSequence (CString name, RecordIndex* rindex, NameIndex* nindex) @arrow{} Sequence*
Returns the Sequence of persons who match a Gedcom name. If the first letter of
the given names is '*', the Sequence will contain all persons who match the surname.

MNOTE: returns a new sequence; caller responsible for its memory.
@end defun

@defun keyToSequence (CString key, RecordIndex* index) @arrow{} Sequence*
Returns a single element Sequence with the record with the given key; or null.
@end defun

@subsection splitjoin.h

@defun splitPerson (GNode* indi, GNode** pname, GNode** prefn, GNode** psex, GNode** pbody, GNode** pfamc, GNode** pfams) @arrow{} void
@defunx joinPerson (GNode* indi, GNode* name, GNode* refn, GNode* sex, GNode* body, GNode* famc, GNode* fams) @arrow{} void
@defunx splitFamily (GNode* fam, GNode** prefn, GNode** phusb, GNode** pwife, GNode** pchil, GNode** prest) @arrow{} void
@defunx joinFamily (GNode* fam, GNode* refn, GNode* husb, GNode* wife, GNode* chil, GNode* rest) @arrow{} void
@defunx splitSource (GNode* root, GNode** prefn, GNode** pbody) @arrow{} void
@defunx joinSource  (GNode* root, GNode* refn, GNode* body) @arrow{} void
@defunx splitEvent (GNode* root, GNode** prefn, GNode** pbody) @arrow{} void
@defunx joinEvent (GNode* root, GNode* refn, GNode* body) @arrow{} void
@defunx splitOther (GNode* root, GNode** prefn, GNode** pbody) @arrow{} void
@defunx joinOther (GNode* root, GNode* refn, GNode* body) @arrow{} void

These functions are used to place the nodes within a record into
canonical order.  These functions are usually called in two
circumstances.  First, when a GEDCOM file is first read in to create a
database.  And second, when a record is edited or added.
@end defun

@defun normalizePerson (GNode*) @arrow{} GNode*
@defunx normalizeFamily (GNode*) @arrow{} GNode*
@defunx normalizeSource (GNode*) @arrow{} GNode*
@defunx normalizeEvent (GNode*) @arrow{} GNode*
@defunx normalizeOther (GNode*) @arrow{} GNode*
Simplified interface to the corresponding split / join function.  It
calls the corresponding split function and then calls the join
function on the result.
@end defun

@defun normalizeRecord (GNode* node) @arrow{} GNode*
An even more simplified interface to the split / join functions.  It
looks at the @code{tag} of the GNode @code{node}, calls the
appropriate pair of split / join functions, and returns the result.
@end defun

@subsection writenode.h / writenode.c

@defun writeGNodeRecord (FILE* fp, GNode* root, bool indent) @arrow{} void
Write the record rooted at @code{root} to the stdio stream @code{fp}.
If @code{indent} is true, then each level is indented relative to the
the previous level.  This is visually useful when editing.

When exporting a database, this function would typically be called
iteratively on all the @code{root} nodes within the database.  When
exporting, @code{indent} should be @code{false}.

When exporting, you'll also need to write a header and a trailer.
@end defun

@defun writeGNodes (FILE*, int level, GNode*, bool indent, bool kids, bool sibs) @arrow{} void
Writes a GNode tree or forest to a file. @code{level} is the current
level; @code{indent} specifies indenting; @code{kids} and @code{sibs}
indicate recursing to children and siblings.
@end defun

@defun writeGNode (FILE*, int level, GNode*, bool indent) @arrow{} void
Writes a single GNode to a file. @code{level} is the node's level;
@code{indent} specifies whether to indent the lines.
@end defun

@defun gnodesToFile (int level, GNode* gnode, CString fileName, bool indent) @arrow{} bool
Writes a GNode tree or forest to a file. Opens the file, calls
@code{writeGNodes} to write the nodes, and then closes the file.
Returns whether the write occurred.

Parameters:
@itemize
@item @code{level} is the level of the top GNode.
@item @code{gnode} is the root
@item @code{fileName} is the file name
@item @code{indent} indicates whether the lines should be indented.
@end itemize
@end defun

@defun treeStringLength (int, GNode*) @arrow{} int
Computes the string length of a @code{GNode} forest.  Length returned
does @b{NOT} include the terminating NULL byte.
@end defun

@defun gnodeToString (GNode*, int level) @arrow{} String
Returns a @code{GNode} as a GEDCOM string without a terminating newline.

It is the callers responsibility to free the string when done with it.
@end defun

@defun gnodesToString (GNode*) @arrow{} String
Returns a @code{GNode} tree converted to a string Finds the length of
the string (calls @code{treeStringLength}), allocates it, fills it
with the GEDCOM text, and returns it.

It is the callers responsibility to free the string when done with it.
@end defun

